Non-Equilibrium Plasma Catalysis for Trace Contaminant Oxidation in Sealed Mars Habitats


Author: Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

Human habitation of Mars requires closed-loop air revitalization capable of operating for >500 days without terrestrial resupply. Conventional trace contaminant control technologies—thermal catalytic oxidation and activated carbon adsorption—face prohibitive power and mass penalties at Mars habitat pressures (60–70 kPa total, 21 kPa O₂ partial pressure, balance CO₂). Non-thermal plasma (NTP) coupled with heterogeneous catalysis offers a low-temperature, regenerable alternative. However, the entire NTP-VOC remediation literature addresses operation at 1 atm in air or N₂ backgrounds; the reduced-pressure CO₂/O₂/H₂O regime characteristic of a Mars habitat alters electron kinetics, radical transport, and surface chemistry in ways not captured by existing models.

This dissertation presents a complete theoretical and computational architecture for a Mars-relevant plasma-catalytic air revitalization system. No physical experiments beyond the electrostatic precipitator (ESP) validation at 600 Pa described in Chapter 6 have been conducted, and no machine learning models have been trained on empirical data. All performance predictions, control strategies, and design specifications are derived from first-principles plasma kinetics, multi-scale fluid modeling, global sensitivity analysis, and rigorous control theory. The work establishes formal mathematical proofs bridging electron kinetics to macroscopic observables, radical transport to catalyst coupling, and plasma stochasticity to control-theoretic safety guarantees.

Key contributions include: (i) derivation of the G-value scaling law for ternary CO₂/O₂/H₂O mixtures, incorporating electronegative attachment filtering; (ii) a corrected surface Damköhler analysis proving >70% radical delivery efficiency at 600 Pa; (iii) a bond valence sum–based mechanistic justification for Cs-doped double perovskite catalyst selection; (iv) a multi-scale computational framework with bidirectional gas-surface coupling justified by Onsager reciprocity; (v) a global sensitivity analysis identifying the 18 dominant reactions controlling VOC removal; (vi) a computational trade study selecting post-plasma catalysis (PPC) for 500-day durability; (vii) a Student-t process surrogate with robustness guarantees and a Control Lyapunov-Barrier Function (CLBF) safety layer; and (viii) a complete hardware architecture with mass, power, and validation roadmap aligned with NASA BLiSS and ESA MELiSSA requirements.

The work advances the technology from TRL 2 to TRL 3 (component validation in relevant environment) and defines a clear, prioritized experimental path to TRL 6.

