Chapter 1 — Introduction and Methodological Boundaries

1.1 The Mars Pressure Gap and Realistic Habitat Atmosphere

Human habitation of Mars requires closed-loop environmental control and life support systems (ECLSS) capable of operating reliably for mission durations exceeding 500 days without terrestrial resupply. While the Martian ambient surface pressure averages approximately 600 Pa with a composition dominated by CO₂ (95.3%), N₂ (2.7%), Ar (2.0%), and trace O₂, the interior of a crewed habitat will be pressurized to 60–70 kPa total pressure for crew physiology and fire safety, with a 21 kPa O₂ partial pressure. The balance gas will be predominantly CO₂ extracted from the Martian atmosphere, resulting in a ternary mixture of CO₂, O₂, and H₂O vapor at reduced total pressure relative to Earth, with CO₂ concentrations far exceeding terrestrial indoor environments. The design target adopted herein is 0.5% CO₂ (300 Pa partial pressure), consistent with NASA Spacecraft Maximum Allowable Concentration (SMAC) for 500‑day exposure; the 1.0% upper bound occasionally cited in literature is not a safe operational target and is not used.

At these pressures, conventional trace contaminant control technologies face fundamental thermodynamic and logistical barriers. Thermal catalytic oxidizers require prohibitive power (300–750 W) to maintain catalyst bed temperatures above light‑off thresholds due to reduced heat capacity and increased radiative losses at low pressure. Activated carbon cartridges, while effective at atmospheric pressure, become logistically unsustainable for long‑duration missions: a 500‑day Mars surface mission would require approximately 60 kg of activated carbon per crew member for trace contaminant removal alone. Furthermore, volatile organic compounds (VOCs)—including formaldehyde (HCHO), benzene (C₆H₆), and ammonia (NH₃)—accumulate rapidly in sealed environments, breaching NASA SMAC thresholds within weeks and poisoning downstream CO₂ scrubbers such as zeolite beds or solid amine systems.

Non-thermal plasma (NTP) offers a compelling alternative: it generates reactive oxygen and hydroxyl radicals (O, OH, HO₂) at near‑room temperature via electron‑impact dissociation, oxidizing VOCs to CO₂ and H₂O without bulk heating. However, the entire body of NTP‑VOC remediation literature addresses operation at 1 atm in air or N₂ backgrounds. The reduced‑pressure CO₂/O₂/H₂O regime characteristic of a Mars habitat alters every aspect of plasma physics and chemistry:

· The electron energy distribution function (EEDF) shifts toward lower mean energies due to strong vibrational excitation cross‑sections in CO₂ below 2 eV and dissociative attachment to O₂ at the 6.7 eV resonance.
· Radical diffusivity increases approximately 170‑fold relative to 1 atm (see Theorem 3.4), fundamentally changing transport timescales.
· Three‑body collision frequencies for ozone formation and radical recombination decrease with pressure, altering steady‑state species concentrations.
· The Paschen breakdown curve shifts, and discharge stability is affected by electronegative impurities.

Recent 2025–2026 literature has advanced CO₂ plasma kinetics for ISRU applications (e.g., updated cross‑sections by Liu et al., 2025) and general NTP‑VOC abatement at 1 atm, but no integrated model exists for 600 Pa CO₂/O₂/H₂O mixtures with trace VOCs. Guerra et al. (2021, 2025) have studied admixture effects (Ar, N₂) on CO₂ vibrational kinetics, but habitat‑relevant O₂ and H₂O impurities introduce electronegative attachment and OH production pathways not previously modeled at reduced pressure. This dissertation addresses that gap directly: at 600 Pa, radical diffusivity rises ~170× while three‑body rates drop sharply, shifting the system from recombination‑dominated (1 atm) to diffusion/wall‑dominated regimes. This favors plasma‑catalysis synergy but requires new scaling laws derived herein.

1.2 Research Objectives

This dissertation pursues the following objectives:

RO1. Derive and prove the mathematical foundations governing electron kinetics in CO₂/O₂/H₂O mixtures, including the effect of dissociative attachment on the EEDF and the resulting pressure‑scaling law for the G‑value.

RO2. Construct a multi‑scale computational framework coupling 0‑D plasma kinetics with impurity chemistry, 2‑D axisymmetric radical transport with bidirectional surface coupling justified by Onsager reciprocal relations, and surface heterogeneous catalysis.

RO3. Perform global sensitivity analysis on an expanded 312‑reaction mechanism to isolate the dominant pathways controlling VOC removal and O₃ formation under realistic Mars habitat gas composition.

RO4. Conduct a computational trade study comparing in‑plasma catalysis (IPC) and post‑plasma catalysis (PPC) architectures at 600 Pa, and provide first‑principles justification for catalyst selection using bond valence sum analysis and recent literature on Cs‑doped double perovskites.

RO5. Specify a complete hardware architecture including ESP (validated at 600 Pa with simulant dust), DBD/catalyst stage, α‑MnO₂ monolith, and moisture‑managed Hopcalite/MOF‑74 carbon loop closure, with full mass, power, and pressure‑drop calculations.

RO6. Derive a PCA‑enhanced Student‑t process surrogate with formal robustness guarantees, and a Soft Actor‑Critic (SAC) controller with Control Barrier Function (CBF) safety layer, supported by a Control Lyapunov‑Barrier Function (CLBF) stability conjecture.

RO7. Establish predictive performance bounds, theoretical TRL trajectory, and a complete experimental validation roadmap aligned with NASA BLiSS and ESA MELiSSA requirements.

1.3 Scope and Methodological Boundaries

This dissertation is strictly theoretical and computational, with the single exception of the ESP validation experiment described in Chapter 6. No physical plasma‑catalytic reactor has been fabricated beyond the ESP test article, and no machine learning models have been trained on empirical data. All results derive from analytical derivations, numerical modeling specifications, and systems engineering design. The work establishes the mathematical proofs, computational architecture, and hardware specifications required to guide future experimental campaigns. Validation protocols, test matrices, and synthetic data training pipelines are explicitly detailed in Chapter 9 as the immediate next phase of research.

The computational framework uses established software tools (ZDPlasKin, BOLSIG+, COMSOL, CHEMKIN) with cross‑section data from LXCat. All input files and post‑processing scripts are archived for reproducibility. The control surrogate is trained exclusively on synthetic data generated by the validated multi‑scale model, with domain randomization to prepare for future hardware transfer. No claims are made about experimental plasma‑catalytic performance beyond what is predicted by the first‑principles models.

This explicit scoping ensures that the dissertation's contributions—mathematical proofs, scaling laws, and computational architecture—stand on their own intellectual merit and provide a rigorous foundation for subsequent experimental validation.

Chapter 2 — Literature Review and Theoretical Context

2.1 Non-Thermal Plasma for VOC Remediation

Plasma-based VOC abatement at 1 atm in air or N₂ backgrounds is well-characterized in the literature. Dielectric barrier discharge (DBD) reactors achieve G-values (molecules removed per 100 eV input) of 5–10 for formaldehyde and 3–6 for benzene under standard conditions. Extensive kinetic modeling efforts, notably by the Eindhoven University of Technology and LAPLACE laboratory groups, have validated 0-D global models against experiments for humid air conditions (Van Gaens & Bogaerts, 2013). The dominant VOC oxidation pathways involve O(³P) and OH radicals abstracting hydrogen or adding to aromatic rings, with secondary reactions of peroxy and alkoxy radicals leading to complete mineralization.

However, the extrapolation of these 1 atm air results to the Mars habitat environment (600 Pa, 95% CO₂) is non-trivial. The three-body reaction rates that dominate radical termination at 1 atm (e.g., O + O₂ + M → O₃ + M) scale with the square of pressure, reducing their contribution by a factor of approximately 28,600 at 600 Pa. This shifts the system dynamics toward diffusion-dominated wall losses, a regime that is poorly represented in the terrestrial NTP-VOC literature.

2.2 CO₂ Plasma Physics at Reduced Pressure

CO₂ plasma physics at pressures relevant to Mars ISRU (100–1000 Pa) has been studied extensively for O₂ production via CO₂ dissociation. Key findings relevant to this work include:

· EEDF Non-Maxwellian Behavior: The electron energy distribution function in pure CO₂ is strongly non-Maxwellian, with a pronounced dip in the 2–4 eV range due to vibrational excitation resonances (Phelps, 1999; Itikawa, 2002). The updated 2025 cross-section compilation by Liu et al. refines the dissociation branching ratio for the 7 eV channel, which is critical for accurate O-atom production predictions.
· Streamer-to-Glow Transition: The transition to a glow discharge occurs at lower pd values in CO₂ than in N₂. Correction: This is not due to higher secondary emission coefficients, as previously misstated in some literature. CO₂ has a lower secondary emission coefficient (\gamma \approx 0.001\text{--}0.005) than N₂ (\gamma \approx 0.01\text{--}0.05). The transition is driven by a higher ionization coefficient \alpha/N at moderate reduced electric fields (Phelps, 1999).
· Impurity Sensitivity: Guerra et al. (2021, 2025) demonstrated that admixtures of 2% Ar and 2% N₂ alter vibrational temperatures by more than 30% relative to pure CO₂ predictions. Electron attachment to O₂ impurities—present even at ppm levels—acts as a high-pass filter on the EEDF tail, significantly altering electron density and radical production. This underscores the necessity of impurity-inclusive modeling for Mars habitat atmospheres, which contain both O₂ and H₂O.

2.3 Plasma-Catalytic Synergy and Perovskite Catalysts

Double perovskites (A₂B′B″O₆) exhibit superior catalytic activity for VOC oxidation compared to single perovskites (ABO₃) due to enhanced oxygen mobility and redox cycling between B-site cations. La₂CoMnO₆ specifically demonstrates high activity for aromatic ring-opening and CO oxidation. Recent work on A-site doping with Cs (La₁.₇Cs₀.₃CoMnO₆₋δ) shows a 4.5-fold enhancement in lattice oxygen reactivity via optimized Co/Mn 3d–O 2p orbital hybridization (Kim et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025).

The Mars-van Krevelen (MvK) mechanism governs VOC oxidation on perovskite surfaces: lattice oxygen reacts with adsorbed VOC, creating an oxygen vacancy that is subsequently replenished by gas-phase oxygen or plasma-generated radicals. The rate of this redox cycle determines both catalytic activity and long-term stability under plasma exposure. Cs doping compresses the [Co/MnO₆] octahedra, weakening metal–oxygen bonds and lowering the activation energy for oxygen migration, which is critical for sustaining activity in the plasma afterglow.

2.4 Electrostatic Precipitation at Low Pressure

The Deutsch-Anderson model, with Cunningham slip correction, predicts improved collection efficiency at reduced pressure due to decreased gas viscosity and increased ion mobility. Recent 2025 studies on Martian dust ESPs using corona discharge (Calle et al., 2025) reveal that electrohydrodynamic forces dominate collection at low flow speeds. The validation experiment conducted for this dissertation (Chapter 6) confirms that the model holds reasonably well for JSC Mars-1 simulant at 600 Pa, with triboelectric charge variations introducing minor discrepancies.

2.5 Machine Learning for Safety-Critical Plasma Control

Gaussian Processes (GPs) provide principled uncertainty quantification for surrogate modeling of plasma systems, but their squared-exponential kernel makes them sensitive to outliers such as discharge instabilities (arcs). Student-t processes generalize GPs by replacing Gaussian marginals with heavy-tailed Student-t distributions, providing automatic robustness (Shah et al., 2014). Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) with Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) has been successfully deployed in tokamak density control and combustion instability suppression, demonstrating formal safety guarantees while optimizing performance. Domain randomization during offline training has proven effective for bridging sim-to-real gaps (Tobin et al., 2017).

2.6 Identified Gaps in the Literature

The review of the current state of the art reveals the following specific gaps that this dissertation addresses:

1. No integrated kinetic model exists for 600 Pa CO₂/O₂/H₂O mixtures representative of Mars habitat atmospheres.
2. No computational trade study compares In-Plasma Catalysis (IPC) vs. Post-Plasma Catalysis (PPC) architectures for Mars-relevant pressure and gas composition.
3. Catalyst durability under extended DBD exposure at reduced pressure is undocumented.
4. Formal mathematical proofs connecting electron kinetics to G-value scaling, and plasma stochasticity to control-theoretic safety, are absent.
5. Pressure gap analysis: While 2025 cross-section updates and Cs-doping studies provide supporting pieces, no prior work integrates impurity-aware EEDF filtering, low-pressure Damköhler transport, BVS-justified catalyst optimization, and CLBF safety proofs into a complete Mars-habitat architecture.

