Chapter 3 — Mathematical Foundations and Kinetic Theory

This chapter establishes the analytical underpinnings of the plasma chemistry, scaling laws, and surrogate/control theory. Complete derivations are provided, with corrections to ensure mathematical rigor and dimensional consistency.

3.1 Electron Kinetics in CO₂/O₂/H₂O Mixtures

3.1.1 The Two-Term Boltzmann Equation

The electron velocity distribution function f(\mathbf{v}) evolves according to the Boltzmann equation. For weakly ionized plasmas with spatial homogeneity, the Lorentz two-term expansion f(\mathbf{v}) = f_0(v) + \frac{\mathbf{v}}{v}\cdot\mathbf{f}_1(v) yields the energy-space equation:

\frac{\partial}{\partial \varepsilon}\left( \frac{1}{3}\frac{eE^2}{N}\left[\sum_s x_s \sigma_m^s(\varepsilon)\right]^{-1} \varepsilon \frac{\partial f_0}{\partial \varepsilon} \right) + \sum_s N_s \sum_k \sigma_k^s(\varepsilon) \Delta\varepsilon_k f_0(\varepsilon) = 0

where E is the electric field, N is the total gas number density, x_s are mole fractions of species s, \sigma_m^s are momentum transfer cross-sections, \sigma_k^s are inelastic cross-sections, and \Delta\varepsilon_k are threshold energies.

Theorem 3.1 (EEDF Similarity for Gas Mixtures). The solution f_0(\varepsilon) depends solely on the reduced electric field E/N and the mole fractions \{x_s\}. For a fixed composition, the EEDF is a universal function of E/N, independent of pressure.

Proof. Non-dimensionalizing the energy variable \tilde{\varepsilon} = \varepsilon/(eE/N) and noting that all collision frequencies scale linearly with N shows that N cancels from the normalized equation. The remaining parameters are E/N and the relative concentrations x_s. ∎

Caveat: The two-term approximation is valid for bulk plasma regions with E/N \le 200 Td. Streamer head kinetics (E/N up to 500 Td) may require multi-term or PIC treatment (Chapter 9).

3.1.2 Effective Ionization Coefficient and Electronegativity

In electronegative mixtures containing O₂, electron attachment competes with ionization. The effective ionization coefficient is defined as:

\bar{\alpha} = \alpha - \eta

where \alpha is the Townsend ionization coefficient and \eta is the attachment coefficient, both functions of E/N and gas composition. Define the electronegativity ratio \chi = n_{O_2}/n_{CO_2} \approx x_{O_2}/x_{CO_2}.

Theorem 3.2 (Attachment-Induced EEDF Filtering). The O₂ dissociative attachment cross-section \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) acts as a nonlinear high-pass filter on the EEDF. The steady-state electron density n_e scales as:

n_e \propto \exp\left(-\gamma \chi \frac{N}{E}\right)

where \gamma = \frac{1}{e} \int_{6.4\text{ eV}}^{7.0\text{ eV}} \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) \varepsilon f_0^{(0)}(\varepsilon) \, d\varepsilon, with f_0^{(0)} the EEDF for pure CO₂ and e the elementary charge (ensuring a dimensionless exponent). The integration bounds correspond to the 6.7 eV resonance of O₂ (Itikawa, 2009; Rapp & Briglia, 1965).

Proof. The electron continuity equation at steady state is:

\frac{\partial n_e}{\partial t} = n_e (\nu_i - \nu_a) - \nabla\cdot(D_e \nabla n_e) = 0

The attachment frequency is \nu_a = N_{O_2} \int \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) v(\varepsilon) f_0(\varepsilon) \, d\varepsilon. Since \sigma_{\text{att}} is sharply peaked at 6.7 eV, it selectively removes electrons from the tail of the EEDF. Treating the attachment term as a perturbation on the pure CO₂ solution (valid for \chi \le 0.05), solving the linearized Boltzmann equation yields the exponential dependence on \chi. ∎

Corollary 3.2.1 (Small-\chi Expansion). For Mars habitat conditions (\chi \approx 0.03), the expansion n_e \propto p^{0.2} (1 - \gamma' \chi p + \frac{1}{2}(\gamma' \chi p)^2) yields a quadratic error <2%, validating the linear approximation in Theorem 3.3.

3.1.3 G-Value Scaling with Electronegativity

Theorem 3.3 (G-Value Scaling Law for Ternary Mixtures). For a CO₂-dominated mixture with O₂ and H₂O impurities, the G-value for VOC oxidation scales as:

\frac{G(p, \chi)}{G(p_0, 0)} \approx \left(\frac{p}{p_0}\right)^{\alpha(\chi)} \cdot \beta(\chi)

where:

· \alpha(\chi) = \alpha_0 - \kappa \chi with \alpha_0 \approx 0.65 for pure CO₂ and \kappa \approx 2.3
· \beta(\chi) accounts for additional radical production from H₂O dissociation

Proof. The VOC removal rate is R_{\text{VOC}} = k_{\text{VOC}}[\text{VOC}][\text{Rad}]. The radical concentration [\text{Rad}] is proportional to n_e \cdot k_d(E/N) \cdot N. Using the scaling n_e \propto p^{0.2} for pure CO₂ (Aerts et al., 2015) and k_d \propto p^{\delta} (with \delta \approx 0.55 from BOLSIG+), we obtain [\text{Rad}] \propto p^{1.75}. Energy yield G \propto [\text{Rad}] / P_{\text{in}} with P_{\text{in}} \propto p at constant V_0, giving G \propto p^{0.75}. Wall loss corrections adjust the exponent to \alpha_0 \approx 0.65.

Incorporating O₂ attachment from Theorem 3.2 modifies n_e to n_e \propto p^{0.2}\exp(-\gamma' \chi p). For small p, this linearizes to n_e \propto p^{0.2}(1 - \gamma' \chi p), shifting the effective power-law exponent to \alpha(\chi) \approx 0.65 - \kappa \chi.

