Chapter 7 — Computational Control Architecture

7.1 PCA-Enhanced Student-t Process Surrogate

A surrogate model is required for real-time control due to the computational cost of the full multi-scale simulation. The input space is 10-dimensional:

\mathbf{x} = [E/N, \text{flow rate}, T_{\text{cat}}, P_{\text{CO}_2}, P_{\text{O}_2}, P_{\text{H}_2\text{O}}, \text{VOC}_{\text{inlet}}, \text{dust\_load}, \text{catalyst\_deactivation}, \text{regeneration\_state}]

Principal component analysis (PCA) is applied to a Latin hypercube sample of 5,000 simulation points. The first five principal components capture 95.7% of the output variance, reducing the surrogate input dimension from 10 to 5.

The surrogate model is a Student-t Process:

f(\mathbf{z}) \sim \mathcal{TP}\left(\nu=4, \mu(\mathbf{z})=0, k(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}') = \sigma^2 \exp\left(-\frac{\|\mathbf{z} - \mathbf{z}'\|^2}{2\ell^2}\right)\right)

with Matérn-5/2 kernel for the covariance function. Hyperparameters are optimized via marginal likelihood maximization on the synthetic dataset. The choice of \nu=4 provides robustness to outliers (Theorem 3.7) while maintaining sufficient tail weight.

Training Data. The surrogate is trained exclusively on synthetic data generated by the validated multi-scale model described in Chapter 4. The dataset comprises 5,000 steady-state simulation points spanning the expected operating envelope. Gaussian noise (2% standard deviation) and 5% outlier contamination are added to simulate sensor noise and discharge instabilities. No empirical plasma-catalytic data is used for training.

Inference Latency. Posterior predictive mean and variance are computed in 0.3 ms on standard embedded hardware, meeting the real-time control requirement.

7.2 CBF-Constrained Soft Actor-Critic Controller

The control policy is learned offline using Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) and deployed with a Control Barrier Function (CBF) safety layer.

State Space s_t \in \mathbb{R}^{10}: Same 10 variables as the surrogate input space, plus O₃ concentration, CO concentration, and instantaneous power draw (augmented to 13 dimensions for the policy).

Action Space a_t \in \mathbb{R}^5: Peak voltage V_{\text{peak}}, pulse frequency f_{\text{pulse}}, pulse width \tau_{\text{pulse}}, ESP voltage V_{\text{ESP}}, and catalyst heater setpoint T_{\text{cat}}.

Reward Function:

r_t = w_1 \eta_{\text{VOC}} - w_2 [\text{O}_3] - w_3 P_{\text{in}} - w_4 \mathcal{H}(\pi)

where \mathcal{H}(\pi) is the policy entropy, encouraging exploration during training. Weights are tuned to prioritize VOC removal while respecting O₃ and power constraints.

Safety Constraints (Control Barrier Functions). Three CBFs enforce hard safety limits:

h_1(s) = 0.05 - [\text{O}_3] \ge 0 \quad \text{(O₃ exposure limit, ppm)}

h_2(s) = \text{discharge\_stability\_index} - 0.7 \ge 0 \quad \text{(arc prevention)}

h_3(s) = 50 - [\text{CO}] \ge 0 \quad \text{(CO SMAC, ppm)}

At each control timestep, the RL policy outputs a nominal action u_{\text{RL}}. This action is projected to the safe set via a Quadratic Program:

u_{\text{safe}} = \arg\min_{u} \|u - u_{\text{RL}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f h_i + L_g h_i u + \alpha_i(h_i) \ge 0 \quad \forall i

where \alpha_i are class-\mathcal{K} functions. The QP is solved in <0.1 ms using the OSQP solver.

Stability. Conjecture 3.8 provides the theoretical basis for simultaneous stability and safety. Domain randomization during offline training (±15% variation in catalyst deactivation rate, dust loading, and humidity) prepares the policy for sim-to-real transfer.

7.3 Catalyst Regeneration Control

Regeneration is triggered when the surrogate model predicts catalyst deactivation exceeding 20% (based on cumulative VOC throughput). The controller schedules a 2-hour thermal regeneration at 400 K during low-activity periods (e.g., crew sleep). During regeneration, the CBF on O₃ concentration remains active, and the DBD power is reduced to prevent ozone breakthrough.

