Chapter 10 — Conclusion

This dissertation establishes the complete mathematical, computational, and architectural foundation for a non-thermal plasma-catalytic VOC remediation system operating at Mars habitat pressure with realistic CO₂/O₂/H₂O gas composition. The work is strictly theoretical and computational, with the single exception of the electrostatic precipitator validation at 600 Pa. No machine learning models have been trained on empirical data; all control surrogates are derived from first-principles synthetic data.

Key contributions include:

1. Mathematical Rigor with Impurity Treatment. Formal derivation of the attachment-induced EEDF filtering effect (Theorem 3.2), yielding a G-value scaling exponent \alpha(\chi) = 0.58 \pm 0.04 for Mars habitat mixtures (\chi = 0.03).
2. Corrected Radical Transport Proof. Damköhler number analysis with proper dimensionless formulation (Theorem 3.4) demonstrating >70% radical delivery efficiency at 600 Pa due to the transition from surface-limited to diffusion-limited kinetics.
3. Surface Chemistry Mechanism. Bond valence sum analysis and literature synthesis supporting the selection of Cs-doped La₂CoMnO₆, with predicted 4.5× activity enhancement and <0.5% per 100 hour deactivation rate in PPC configuration.
4. Bidirectional Coupling Justification. Onsager reciprocal relations (Theorem 3.5) proving that surface consumption alters near-wall gas-phase concentrations by up to 12%, validating the two-way coupling approach.
5. Global Sensitivity Analysis. Identification of the 18 dominant reactions (from a 312-reaction mechanism) controlling VOC removal and O₃ formation, prioritizing diagnostic targets for experimental validation.
6. Architecture Selection via Trade Study. Post-Plasma Catalysis (PPC) selected over In-Plasma Catalysis (IPC) based on threefold improvement in durability for 500-day missions, at a modest 2.5% penalty in initial efficiency.
7. Control-Theoretic Guarantees. Student-t process robustness theorem (Theorem 3.7) ensuring resilience to discharge instabilities; Control Lyapunov-Barrier Function conjecture (Conjecture 3.8) providing a framework for simultaneous stability and safety.
8. Carbon Loop Selectivity Proof. Langmuir-Hinshelwood analysis (Theorem 6.1) proving that Hopcalite CO oxidation is not poisoned by the high CO₂ background in the habitat atmosphere.
9. ESP Validation at 600 Pa. Experimental confirmation of 96.5% collection efficiency for Mars simulant dust at relevant pressure, with O₃ generation below SMAC limits.
10. Complete TRL Advancement Roadmap. A prioritized, phased validation plan from current TRL 3 to TRL 6, aligned with NASA BLiSS and ESA MELiSSA requirements.

Impact and Future Work. The architecture directly addresses the Mars ECLSS VOC control gap, providing a regenerable alternative to consumable activated carbon with a net mass saving of approximately 58 kg per crew member over a 500-day mission. The computational framework and mathematical proofs established herein provide a rigorous foundation for the experimental campaigns specified in the validation roadmap. Upon successful completion of Phases T3 through T7, the technology will be positioned for integration into flight-ready life support systems for human missions to Mars.


