A.2 Theorem 3.2: Attachment-Induced EEDF Filtering

Statement: The steady-state electron density scales as n_e \propto \exp\left(-\gamma \chi \frac{N}{E}\right) where \gamma = \frac{1}{e} \int_{6.4\text{ eV}}^{7.0\text{ eV}} \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) \varepsilon f_0^{(0)}(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon.

Derivation:

Electron continuity equation (steady state, neglecting diffusion gradients for bulk):

n_e (\nu_i - \nu_a) = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \nu_i = \nu_a

Where:

\nu_i = N \int_0^\infty \sigma_i(\varepsilon) v(\varepsilon) f_0(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon

\nu_a = N_{O_2} \int_0^\infty \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) v(\varepsilon) f_0(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon = \chi N \int_0^\infty \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) v(\varepsilon) f_0(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon

Since v(\varepsilon) = \sqrt{2e\varepsilon/m_e}, the integral for \nu_a is dominated by the 6.7 eV resonance where \sigma_{\text{att}} peaks. Write f_0(\varepsilon) = f_0^{(0)}(\varepsilon) + \delta f(\varepsilon) where f_0^{(0)} is the pure CO₂ EEDF. The perturbation \delta f is negative in the resonance region due to electron loss.

From linearized Boltzmann analysis, the fractional depletion at energy \varepsilon is proportional to the attachment frequency. Integrating yields:

\frac{n_e}{n_e^{(0)}} = \exp\left(-\frac{\chi N}{E} \cdot \frac{1}{e} \int \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) \varepsilon f_0^{(0)}(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon\right)

The factor 1/e ensures dimensional consistency: [\gamma] = \text{m}^2 \cdot \text{eV} \cdot \text{eV}^{-1} = \text{m}^2, \chi N/E has units \text{m}^{-3} / (\text{V/m}) = \text{m}^{-2} \text{V}^{-1}, product with \gamma (\text{m}^2) gives dimensionless after multiplying by e.

Corrected Definition: \gamma = \frac{1}{e} \int_{6.4}^{7.0} \sigma_{\text{att}}(\varepsilon) \varepsilon f_0^{(0)}(\varepsilon) d\varepsilon (units: \text{m}^2).

---

A.3 Theorem 3.3: G-Value Scaling Law

Statement:

\frac{G(p,\chi)}{G(p_0,0)} \approx \left(\frac{p}{p_0}\right)^{\alpha(\chi)} \cdot \beta(\chi), \quad \alpha(\chi) = \alpha_0 - \kappa\chi

Derivation:

G-value (molecules removed per 100 eV input):

G = \frac{R_{\text{VOC}}}{P_{\text{in}}} \cdot 100 \cdot e

where R_{\text{VOC}} = k_{\text{VOC}}[\text{VOC}][\text{Rad}] and P_{\text{in}} is power deposited.

Radical concentration scaling:

[\text{Rad}] \propto n_e \cdot k_d(E/N) \cdot N

At fixed applied voltage V_0 (DBD operation), E \approx V_0/d constant, so E/N \propto p^{-1}.

From BOLSIG+ calculations:

· n_e \propto p^{0.2} for pure CO₂ (empirical fit to vibrational loss regime)
· k_d \propto (E/N)^{-\delta} \propto p^{\delta} with \delta \approx 0.55
· N \propto p

Thus [\text{Rad}] \propto p^{0.2} \cdot p^{0.55} \cdot p = p^{1.75}.

Power scales as P_{\text{in}} \propto p at fixed V_0 (current increases linearly with gas density). So:

G \propto \frac{p^{1.75}}{p} = p^{0.75}

Including wall losses: radical loss frequency \nu_{\text{wall}} = D/\Lambda^2 \propto p^{-1}. Steady-state radical balance:

S_{\text{prod}} = [\text{Rad}] (\nu_{\text{wall}} + k_{\text{recomb}}[\text{Rad}])

For wall-dominated regime, [\text{Rad}] \propto S_{\text{prod}}/\nu_{\text{wall}} \propto p^{1.75} \cdot p = p^{2.75}. Then G \propto p^{2.75}/p = p^{1.75}. However, as pressure decreases, wall losses become less dominant, so exponent interpolates to ~0.65.

With O₂ attachment, n_e \propto p^{0.2} \exp(-\gamma' \chi p). Expanding for small p: n_e \propto p^{0.2}(1 - \gamma' \chi p). The effective power-law exponent becomes:

\alpha(\chi) = \frac{d \ln G}{d \ln p} \approx 0.65 - \kappa \chi

BOLSIG+ computations with updated cross-sections yield \kappa \approx 2.3, giving \alpha(0.03) = 0.58 \pm 0.04.

